Dental Caries Dental caries, commonly referred to as tooth decay, is one of the most common of all diseases, second only to the common cold."}, {"sentence_id": "I1:S12", "start": 1301, "end": 1382, "text": "It usually occurs in children and young adults, but any person can experience it."}, {"sentence_id": "I1:S13", "start": 1383, "end": 1434, "text": "It is a common cause of tooth loss in young people."}, {"sentence_id": "I1:S14", "start": 1435, "end": 1598, "text": "Bacterial plaque converts all foods, especially sugar and starches, into acids that subsequently dissolve the enamel surface of the tooth, called demineralization."}, {"sentence_id": "I1:S15", "start": 1599, "end": 1648, "text": "If left untreated, demineralization progresses to"}]}, {"evidence_id": "I2", "title": "Chapter 19: Current Oral Epidemiological Findings", "sentences": [{"sentence_id": "I2:S1", "start": 0, "end": 219, "text": "Chapter 19: Current Oral Epidemiological Findings Chapter 19: Current Oral Epidemiological Findings caries and nursing bottle decay, is a syndrome characterized by severe decay in the teeth of infants or young children."}, {"sentence_id": "I2:S2", "start": 220, "end": 383, "text": "Frequent and prolonged drinking from a bottle (especially at bedtime) increases the risk of this disease, which first affects the maxillary primary anterior teeth."}, {"sentence_id": "I2:S3", "start": 384, "end": 872, "text": "Table 19–3 Percentage of Third Grade Students with Untreated Tooth Decay Sort list by: State Percent with Untreated Tooth Decay State School Year Percent with Untreated Tooth Decay Response Rate1 (%) Percent eligible for the National School Lunch Program2 Sample State Schools3 Students4 Alabama 2005–2007 % CI N 27.6 (25.5–29.7) 9301 73 51 NR 56 Alaska 2007–2008 % CI N 26.25 (23.2–29.3) 826 48 42 NR 46 Arizona 2009–2010 % CI N 40.4 (36.8–44.1) 3150 37 51 NR 48 (continued) 270 UNIT III"}, {"sentence_id": "I2:S4", "start": 873, "end": 1336, "text": "• DENTAL HYGIENE RESEARCH State School Year Percent with Untreated Tooth Decay Response Rate1 (%) Percent eligible for the National School Lunch Program2 Sample State Schools3 Students4 Arkansas 2009–2010 % CI N 29.05 (27.1–30.0) 4239 53 55 NR 65 California 2004–2005 % CI N 28.7 (27.0–30.4) 10444 52 64 NR 57 Colorado 2006–2007 % CI N 24.5 (23.0–26.1) 3012 79 40 NR 41 Connecticut 2006–2007 % CI N 17.8 (14.8–20.8) 8755 81 37 NR 35 Delaware 2001–2002 % CI N 29.9"}]}, {"evidence_id": "I3", "title": "Chapter 4: Dental Hygiene Care Delivery in the Global Community", "sentences": [{"sentence_id": "I3:S1", "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "Chapter 4: Dental Hygiene Care Delivery in the Global Community Chapter 4: Dental Hygiene Care Delivery in the Global Community orientation, play an important role in providing access to oral health care."}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S2", "start": 205, "end": 341, "text": "The majority of the world’s dental hygienists practice in societies that have a high demand for prevention, esthetics, and Did You Know?"}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S3", "start": 342, "end": 427, "text": "Dental caries and periodontal diseases are the most widespread of all human diseases."}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S4", "start": 428, "end": 584, "text": "Another data bank is maintained by the Fédération Dentaire lnternationale (FDI), the organization that represents the international community of dentists.10"}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S5", "start": 585, "end": 758, "text": "In epidemiological evidence in DMFT scores, results showed that existing epidemiological data are inadequate and the studies performed to gather the data are not comparable."}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S6", "start": 759, "end": 925, "text": "No universally accepted criteria appear to be used for the diagnosis of caries, and most international surveys lack interexaminer calibration to ensure consistency.16"}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S7", "start": 926, "end": 1027, "text": "In addition , sampling facilities tend to lack dental chairs, lights, and a supply of compressed air."}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S8", "start": 1028, "end": 1087, "text": "These factors lead to lower estimates of caries experience."}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S9", "start": 1088, "end": 1535, "text": "addition to some epidemiological data on nearly 130 countries , the FDI Web site provides detailed information about dentistry and the dental team in participating member countries including demographics and numbers concerning the dental workforce, institutions for dental and dental hygiene education, type of oral health care system, fluoridation levels, existence of a public health policy, and information on free dental coverage for children."}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S10", "start": 1536, "end": 1758, "text": "However, figures pertaining to the dental hygienist workforce must be viewed with caution, especially for countries in which the profession has not been officially introduced or schools are reported but are not accredited."}, {"sentence_id": "I3:S11", "start": 1759, "end": 1830, "text": "The Council of European Chief Dental Officers (CECDO) maintains another"}]}, {"evidence_id": "I4", "title": "Chapter 19: Current Oral Epidemiological Findings", "sentences": [{"sentence_id": "I4:S1", "start": 0, "end": 179, "text": "Chapter 19: Current Oral Epidemiological Findings Chapter 19: Current Oral Epidemiological Findings subsequently dissolve the enamel surface of the tooth, called demineralization."}, {"sentence_id": "I4:S2", "start": 180, "end": 246, "text": "If left untreated, demineralization progresses to form a “cavity.”"}, {"sentence_id": "I4:S3", "start": 247, "end": 382, "text": "Most recent efforts have focused on the remineralization of demineralized tooth surface, as opposed to restoring the subsequent cavity."}, {"sentence_id": "I4:S4", "start": 383, "end": 519, "text": "EPIDEMIOLOGY Dental caries remains the most prevalent chronic disease in both children and adults even though it is largely preventable."}, {"sentence_id": "I4:S5", "start": 520, 